WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.067
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Laura Dern.

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:05.100
It's such a fantastic book.
There's so many great stories.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.100
Are there any anecdotes
that stand out for you

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.100
that you were really excited
to be able to put on the page

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.634
after you heard them
from your mother?

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:13.534
-I mean, all of my mother's
childhood stories,

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:17.300
again, things I never knew,
stories I had never heard.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.400
But my godmother was the amazing
actress Shelley Winters,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.901
and a lot of their wild rides
are in the book.

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:26.968
I won't spoil them.

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:29.300
There are a few
that aren't in the book,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.634
but hearing
all of their stories --

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:36.033
I remember my mom telling me
this one story of Shelley

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:37.567
had gained weight
and she was like,

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.133
"We've got to go to a spa,
and we've got to lose weight."

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:42.300
And my mother was like,
[sighs]

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.067
Like, "I don't want to really go
or lose weight."

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.534
She's like,
"Well, you're coming with me.

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:47.901
I need you. I need your support.
We're going to go to this place

00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:49.467
and we're going to go
to this weight loss clinic

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:50.934
and it's going
to be incredible."

00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:54.534
And she had this like, old
vintage convertible Cadillac,

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:57.267
and they drove to this place
out in the desert.

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.367
And it was, I think,
the second night my mom

00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:03.167
hears, like, a ferocious
knock at her door

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:04.734
and she says,
"I'm starving to death.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:06.267
You've got to get me
out of here."

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:10.667
And and my mother was like, "No,
this is a five-day program."

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.567
And so she said,
"Just meet me in five minutes

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:13.934
in the parking lot."

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:17.534
And she had put her car
in neutral and made my mom

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:21.601
and she push it carefully
out of the parking lot.

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:24.667
They went for burgers and pizza,

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.434
and then came back
the next morning,

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:27.868
and someone was like,
"I've got to say,

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:29.200
I've had a smoothie
and I'm starving."

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.133
My mom's like, "Really?
I feel fine."

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:34.000
This is -- you know.
-At least she got her exercise

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.501
in pushing a car.
-Exactly.

00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:38.167
Probably break even
at some level.

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.501
-I'm just going to start
doing that and not go.

00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:42.801
-This is very fun.
You get to go on a book tour

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:44.634
and basically road trip
with your mom.

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:46.033
This is the gift
that keeps giving.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.801
-It's incredible.
It's incredible.

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:50.701
We are here together
in New York City doing press.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.868
And I mean, this is her city

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.133
because she loves being
a theater actress as well.

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:59.033
So we're having so much fun
and we're going to Nashville,

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:02.667
San Francisco, New Orleans.
I mean, it's going to be great.

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:05.767
-The whole thing
is is really exciting.

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:08.334
And one thing, you know, I
felt -- I was saying backstage,

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:10.033
this made me feel like

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.701
I should have more conversations
like this with my mother,

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.400
because I think ultimately,
you read this,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.000
and I feel like people who maybe
miss that chance wonder

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.067
why they never asked
these things. Because I do think

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.767
that once you get past
the hurdle of asking them,

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:24.501
everybody wants
to tell their story.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:27.634
And is that part of it,
not just to read the stories

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.334
about your mother/daughter
relationship,

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:31.868
but sort of encourage everybody
to connect with a parent?

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:33.501
-It's the only reason
we're sharing,

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.400
is because I watched my mom,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.634
who I was sure was going
to leave a few years ago,

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:41.400
still be here.
I have actual concrete proof

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.033
that communicating and loving
each other in this way,

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.234
having these conversations

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:48.100
are going to do
so much for everyone.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.734
And so we said,
"Let's just share it."

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:55.234
If it impacts one person --
and we just met someone today

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:57.033
who's a friend who shared
the story

00:02:57.033 --> 00:03:00.467
of going to see his grandmother,
who's very religious,

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:01.634
and as a gay man,

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.734
he was thinking of not
sharing with her

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:07.033
and coming out to her
about his life.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.534
And he thought -- he said,
"I read the book

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.667
and I chose to share
with my grandmother."

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.200
And it was so healing
and so incredible

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.901
and her response
was so loving and beautiful.

00:03:15.901 --> 00:03:17.734
And so my mom was like,
"See?

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:21.000
See? That's the one person."
I was like,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.601
"Well, hopefully there's a few
more that also connect to it."

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:25.767
But it's for everybody.

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:27.701
Friends who are siblings said,

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:30.267
"My God, we've never
asked each other,

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.100
'How are you doing?
Are you okay?'"

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.100
And I think for me,
one of the great lessons

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.000
was I didn't want to
bring up anything

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.300
that might wound her more.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.734
But everything
we talked through,

00:03:40.734 --> 00:03:44.801
even some of her hardest
heartbreaks as we get into here,

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:47.501
she felt that she got
healthier and healthier

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:51.100
by just releasing certain things
that she'd never talked about.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.501
-Well, it's just really lovely.

00:03:52.501 --> 00:03:54.767
And you are, I should note,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.968
building these moments
with your children as well.

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.934
You got to go
to the beginning

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:00.767
of Taylor Swift's tour.
-Yes.

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:02.934
-Not a very easy ticket.
-No.

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:04.567
-Now, I know you've collaborated
with Taylor,

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:06.100
so you had an in.
-Yes. Yes.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.968
I was very lucky to have an in,
and it was so amazing.

00:04:08.968 --> 00:04:10.834
And Taylor now knows
the kids and wanted them there.

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:13.467
-And you had to obviously
choose your era.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:14.934
What did you guys choose?

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:18.234
-We went all eras pretty much.
We went to the party store

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:20.667
and we honored
all the bedazzling

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:22.834
and all the colors and...

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:24.334
-And here are you
with your children.

00:04:24.334 --> 00:04:25.934
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:04:25.934 --> 00:04:28.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.167
-One and done.
You can tell you have

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:31.534
a film background, Laura,

00:04:31.534 --> 00:04:33.167
because this is such
a well taken photo.

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:36.234
Just, you know...
you got perfect lighting.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:38.167
This is a little bit
more professional.

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:41.901
There we go. There's the gang
there with Taylor.

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:43.834
-It was incredible.

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:45.133
-I mean, it's funny because,
I mean,

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.868
I think a lot of people
would imagine

00:04:47.868 --> 00:04:49.934
that having Laura Dern
as a mom is a very cool thing.

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:51.234
But I imagine
when it's your own kids,

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:54.400
you have to go outside
the Laura Dern bubble

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.434
to really impress them.

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:57.200
I would hope
that Taylor delivered.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.234
-She delivered. But it's --
I mean, guys, [laughs]

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:04.234
I've tried so hard for years
to be cool to them.

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.501
Music taste particularly.
-Yeah.

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:08.467
-I was like, "Guys,
I'm playing you Nirvana.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:12.534
You're nine years old.
This should be a great moment."

00:05:12.534 --> 00:05:15.734
But it does turn around.
Like, once they're teenagers,

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:18.567
and you know, we're celebrating
loving the same artists.

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:20.601
-And do they remember
that you tried

00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:22.467
when they were nine,
or did they completely forget?

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:23.901
Or do they come to you
and they're like, "Mom,

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:25.300
why didn't you tell me
about Nirvana?"

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.067
Do you just have to be like,
"I tried and you were dumb?"

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:29.167
-I mean, I will say,

00:05:29.167 --> 00:05:31.801
I remember the moment
when their dad,

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:35.734
my ex, Ben Harper, said,
"Trust me, as a rock musician,

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.834
you think you're going
to be a cool dad.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:41.200
They destroy me every day."
It's just -- You don't win.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.934
-Well, there you go.
Well, you won a little there.

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:44.234
You take it
when you can get it.

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:45.334
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:46.734
What a delight to see you.
-I love it.

00:05:46.734 --> 00:05:48.667
It's so good to see you.
-Laura Dern.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.767
"Honey, Baby, Mine,"
available now

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.701
wherever you buy your books,
but please support local

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:54.300
and independent book stores.
Be right back

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.801
with Miranda Lambert.

